## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-store writes with explicit TTLs and cache invalidation. It checks how writes propagate across Redis, MongoDB, Cassandra, and DynamoDB, plus how to keep a hot cache coherent.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-store write paths with idempotence
- TTL-based bucketing in Cassandra
- Cache invalidation and refresh strategies in Redis
- Read path with locality and fallback

## Code Example
```javascript
async function writeMessage(chatId, userId, text, ts){
  await redis.hset(`chat:presence:${userId}`, 'online', 1);
  const msgId = generateId();
  await mongo.messages.insertOne({chat_id: chatId, msg_id: msgId, sender_id: userId, ts, text});
  await cassandra.execute("INSERT INTO messages_by_chat (chat_id, bucket, ts, msg_id) VALUES (?, bucket(?), ?, ?)", [chatId, ts, ts, msgId]);
  await dynamo.archive({chat_id: chatId, msg_id: msgId, ts, text});
  await redis.publish(`chat:updates:${chatId}`, JSON.stringify({msg_id: msgId}));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of cross-datastore idempotence and failure handling in real-time ingestion, including correct sequencing, conflict resolution, and replay-safe patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotent ingestion across MongoDB, Redis, Cassandra, DynamoDB
- Upserts vs transactional writes across stores
- Idempotency keys, deduplication, and replay handling
- Write-ahead logging and robust retry/backoff strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
async function ingest(event){
  const id = event.event_id;
  const exists = await redis.setnx(`evt:${id}`, '1');
  if (!exists) return { status: 'dup' };
  await mongo.upsert('events', { event_id: id }, event.meta);
  await cassandra.batchUpdate('hourly', event);
  await dynamo.update('weekly', event);
  return { status: 'ok' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures where later stores fail but earlier succeeded?
- How would you evolve the schema if per-user per-hour windows change, ensuring backward compatibility?
